WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.420
Sodium that a blocked door missed flowed on, and cost potassium downstream.

2
00:00:05.700 --> 00:00:10.880
But furosemide also raises urinary calcium, and its door carries no calcium.

3
00:00:12.200 --> 00:00:13.200
So how?

4
00:00:16.250 --> 00:00:22.170
In 1973, Rocha and Kokko held single rabbit thick limbs on glass pipettes.

5
00:00:23.650 --> 00:00:25.430
Salt left the fluid, but water stayed.

6
00:00:26.770 --> 00:00:29.730
And the lumen sat positive, about 7 mV.

7
00:00:30.650 --> 00:00:36.630
Its door, NKCC2, carries one sodium, one potassium and two chlorides together.

8
00:00:37.850 --> 00:00:39.030
That is no net charge.

9
00:00:40.330 --> 00:00:41.990
So where does the voltage come from?

10
00:00:42.983 --> 00:00:44.543
Follow each ion out.

11
00:00:46.123 --> 00:00:47.823
Sodium leaves toward the blood through the pump.

12
00:00:49.203 --> 00:00:51.723
Chloride leaves toward the blood through chloride channels.

13
00:00:53.423 --> 00:00:55.283
Negative charge has crossed to the blood side.

14
00:00:56.333 --> 00:01:02.713
Now potassium. Tubular fluid holds little of it, compared with sodium and chloride.

15
00:01:04.013 --> 00:01:06.753
So how does this door keep getting the potassium it needs?

16
00:01:12.800 --> 00:01:18.780
It recycles. Most of the potassium goes back into the lumen, through the potassium

17
00:01:18.780 --> 00:01:24.240
channel ROMK. Positive charge returns to the lumen side.

18
00:01:25.300 --> 00:01:29.980
Negative charge goes to the blood, and positive charge goes back to the lumen.

19
00:01:31.200 --> 00:01:33.840
So the lumen ends up a few millivolts positive.

20
00:01:35.100 --> 00:01:37.180
The circuit around the door makes the voltage.

21
00:01:38.333 --> 00:01:41.433
The junctions between these cells let cations through.

22
00:01:42.653 --> 00:01:46.233
The positive lumen pushes them out between the cells, toward the blood.

23
00:01:47.653 --> 00:01:52.193
Calcium and magnesium carry two charges each, so they are pushed twice as hard.

24
00:01:53.217 --> 00:02:00.537
At +7 mV, voltage alone could hold a one-charge ion in the lumen near 77% percent of

25
00:02:00.537 --> 00:02:04.557
its blood level. A two-charge ion, about 59%.

26
00:02:04.557 --> 00:02:07.857
percent. That is a limit, not a measurement.

27
00:02:08.967 --> 00:02:12.847
That is why this segment matters for magnesium most of all.

28
00:02:14.247 --> 00:02:19.187
Textbook estimates credit it with about 60–70% of filtered magnesium.

29
00:02:20.547 --> 00:02:24.287
For calcium, the estimate is about 20–25%.

30
00:02:25.367 --> 00:02:26.867
Here is the one idea.

31
00:02:28.147 --> 00:02:32.447
A door can move ions it never carries, if its segment builds a voltage.

32
00:02:33.867 --> 00:02:37.007
Block that door, and the voltage's passengers move much less.

33
00:02:38.067 --> 00:02:43.747
That is how furosemide, blocking a sodium door, ends up raising urinary calcium.

34
00:02:44.887 --> 00:02:49.667
The proximal tubule also takes back calcium between its cells, more than the thick

35
00:02:49.667 --> 00:02:52.907
limb does. This rule leaves that out.

36
00:02:53.900 --> 00:02:55.860
So look past what a door carries.

37
00:02:57.280 --> 00:03:01.560
Ask what voltage its segment was building, and which ions that voltage moved.
